"atc":null,"mesh":"D015377","uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:28+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"CC1(C[C@@H]1C(=O)N/C(=C\\CCCCSC[C@@H](C(=O)O)N)/C(=O)O)C","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":1985,"bbw":0,"fda":0,"ema":1,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:43:53+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"Cilastatin is indicated, in combination with [imipenem] with or without [relebactam], for the treatment of bacterial infections including respiratory, skin, bone, gynecologic, urinary tract, and intra-abdominal as well as septicemia and endocarditis.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01597","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"FDA Label","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01597","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Compounds which inhibit or antagonize biosynthesis or actions of proteases (ENDOPEPTIDASES).","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0017845.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Cilastatin is a chemical compound which inhibits the human enzyme dehydropeptidase. Renal Dehydropeptidase degrades the antibiotic [imipenem]. Cilastatin is therefore combined intravenously with imipenem in order to protect it from dehydropeptidase and prolong its antibacterial effect. However, cilastatin in and of itself does not have any antibacterial activity. The increased renal excretion of unchanged imipenem appears to prevent proximal tubular necrosis associated with high doses of imipenem.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01597","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"Cilastatin is a renal dehydropeptidase-I inhibitor. Since the antibiotic, imipenem, is hydrolyzed by dehydropeptidase-I, which resides in the brush border of the renal tubule, cilastatin is administered with imipenem to block the metabolism of imipenem.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01597","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Cilastatin is reported by official FDA labeling to be 70% excreted in the urine, however published literature has reported values as high as 98%.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01597","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Cilastatin has a volume of distribution of 14.6-20.1L.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01597","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Cilastatin has a total clearance of 0.2 L/h/kg and a renal clearance of 0.10-0.16 L/h/kg.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01597","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Protein Binding","items":[{"section":"Protein Binding","text":"Cilastatin is plasma protein binding is reported to be 35-40%.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01597","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"The half-life of cilastatin is approximately 1h.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB01597","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[],"manufacturing":[],"structure":tru